Claude Opus 5.5, an AI model made by Anthropic, prompted by Colin
Zwanziger, 25 October 2026. See the AI disclosure in
gist.RealCohesion.index.

The quotient of a type by paths from a fixed interval, and its
iterates.

Let I be a type with two points i₀ and i₁, the endpoints. A path in a
type X is a map γ : I → X, from γ i₀ to γ i₁. The path quotient Q X is
the set coequalizer of the two evaluation maps (I → X) ⇉ X. It is the
set quotient of X by the equivalence relation generated by "is joined
to by a path". The real-cohesive instance takes I = ℝ with endpoints
0 and 1, and is used in gist.RealCohesion.FiniteHeightContinuity,
following GPT-6 Astra's internal real-path formulation of the
countermodel to Shulman's continuity conjecture [1].

We obtain the generated equivalence relation as the propositional,
symmetric, reflexive and transitive closure of TypeTopology's
Relations.SRTclosure. It is formed from finite chains, so no
propositional resizing is involved. The quotient comes from the
assumption set-quotients-exist of Quotient.Type. These are small
quotients: if I and X live in the universe 𝓤, then so does Q X, so
that Q can be iterated without raising universe levels.

Besides the quotient map q X : X → Q X and its universal property, we
need only the action of Q on maps, its iterates Q^ n, the composite
quotient maps q[ n ] : X → Q^ n X and their naturality,

 Q^-map n f (q[ n ] x) ＝ q[ n ] (f x),

which is how an identification at a finite stage of the iterated
quotient is transported along a map.

[1] Internal real-path quotients and the finite-height principle.
    Research notes, 25 October 2026.
    references/C06-internal-real-path-quotients.txt

\begin{code}

{-# OPTIONS --safe --without-K #-}

open import MLTT.Spartan
open import UF.PropTrunc
open import Quotient.Type

module gist.RealCohesion.RealPathQuotient
        (pt : propositional-truncations-exist)
        (sq : set-quotients-exist)
        {𝓤 : Universe}
        (I : 𝓤 ̇ )
        (i₀ i₁ : I)
       where

open import UF.Base
open import UF.Sets
open import UF.Subsingletons
open import Relations.SRTclosure using (module psrt)

open PropositionalTruncation pt
open general-set-quotients-exist sq

\end{code}

The relation "is joined to by a path", and the equivalence relation
that it generates.

\begin{code}

path-between : (X : 𝓤 ̇ ) → X → X → 𝓤 ̇
path-between X x y = Σ γ ꞉ (I → X) , (γ i₀ ＝ x) × (γ i₁ ＝ y)

path-equivalence : (X : 𝓤 ̇ ) → EqRel {𝓤} {𝓤} X
path-equivalence X = psrt.psrt-closure pt (path-between X) ,
                     psrt.psrt-is-equiv-rel pt (path-between X)

\end{code}

The path quotient and its quotient map, which identifies the two
endpoints of every path.

\begin{code}

Q : 𝓤 ̇ → 𝓤 ̇
Q X = X / path-equivalence X

q : (X : 𝓤 ̇ ) → X → Q X
q X = η/ (path-equivalence X)

Q-is-set : (X : 𝓤 ̇ ) → is-set (Q X)
Q-is-set X = /-is-set (path-equivalence X)

q-identifies-endpoints : (X : 𝓤 ̇ ) (γ : I → X) → q X (γ i₀) ＝ q X (γ i₁)
q-identifies-endpoints X γ =
 η/-identifies-related-points (path-equivalence X)
  (psrt.psrt-extension pt (path-between X) (γ i₀) (γ i₁) (γ , refl , refl))

\end{code}

The universal property: a map into a set that identifies the endpoints
of every path factors through the path quotient.

\begin{code}

module _ (X : 𝓤 ̇ )
         {𝓦 : Universe}
         {Y : 𝓦 ̇ }
         (Y-is-set : is-set Y)
         (f : X → Y)
         (f-identifies-endpoints : (γ : I → X) → f (γ i₀) ＝ f (γ i₁))
       where

 private
  f-identifies-related-points : identifies-related-points (path-equivalence X) f
  f-identifies-related-points {x} {x'} =
   psrt.psrt-induction pt (path-between X)
    (λ x x' → f x ＝ f x')
    (λ x x' → Y-is-set)
    (λ x → refl)
    (λ x x' p → p ⁻¹)
    (λ x x' x'' p p' → p ∙ p')
    (λ { x x' (γ , refl , refl) → f-identifies-endpoints γ })
    x x'

 Q-rec : Q X → Y
 Q-rec =
  mediating-map/ (path-equivalence X) Y-is-set f f-identifies-related-points

 Q-rec-computes : (x : X) → Q-rec (q X x) ＝ f x
 Q-rec-computes = universality-triangle/ (path-equivalence X) Y-is-set f
                   f-identifies-related-points

\end{code}

The action of the path quotient on maps.

\begin{code}

Q-map : {X Y : 𝓤 ̇ } → (X → Y) → Q X → Q Y
Q-map {X} {Y} f = Q-rec X (Q-is-set Y) (q Y ∘ f)
                   (λ γ → q-identifies-endpoints Y (f ∘ γ))

Q-map-on-units : {X Y : 𝓤 ̇ } (f : X → Y) (x : X)
               → Q-map f (q X x) ＝ q Y (f x)
Q-map-on-units {X} {Y} f = Q-rec-computes X (Q-is-set Y) (q Y ∘ f)
                            (λ γ → q-identifies-endpoints Y (f ∘ γ))

\end{code}

The iterated path quotient, the composite quotient maps, and their
naturality.

\begin{code}

Q^ : ℕ → 𝓤 ̇ → 𝓤 ̇
Q^ 0        X = X
Q^ (succ n) X = Q (Q^ n X)

q[_] : (n : ℕ) {X : 𝓤 ̇ } → X → Q^ n X
q[ 0 ]      x = x
q[ succ n ] {X} x = q (Q^ n X) (q[ n ] x)

Q^-map : (n : ℕ) {X Y : 𝓤 ̇ } → (X → Y) → Q^ n X → Q^ n Y
Q^-map 0        f = f
Q^-map (succ n) f = Q-map (Q^-map n f)

Q^-map-on-units : (n : ℕ) {X Y : 𝓤 ̇ } (f : X → Y) (x : X)
                → Q^-map n f (q[ n ] x) ＝ q[ n ] (f x)
Q^-map-on-units 0        f x = refl
Q^-map-on-units (succ n) {X} {Y} f x =
 Q-map (Q^-map n f) (q (Q^ n X) (q[ n ] x)) ＝⟨ Q-map-on-units (Q^-map n f)
                                                 (q[ n ] x) ⟩
 q (Q^ n Y) (Q^-map n f (q[ n ] x))         ＝⟨ ap (q (Q^ n Y))
                                                 (Q^-map-on-units n f x) ⟩
 q (Q^ n Y) (q[ n ] (f x))                  ∎

\end{code}
